<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lake Chalco</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lake Chalco was a freshwater lake in the south-east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a>, joined to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-xochimilco">Lake Xochimilco</a> and part of the lake system that included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-texcoco">Lake Texcoco</a>. Its shores were home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalca">Chalca</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-states</a>, and chinampa fields in its shallows helped feed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>. It was drained in the late 19th and early 20th centuries, and much of its former bed is now built over.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-chalco.jpg" alt="Lake Chalco" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AlfaBeta0104 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nuevo_Lago_de_Chalco.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Lake Chalco was an endorheic lake, one with no outlet to the sea, named after the ancient city of Chalco on its eastern shore. With the brackish lakes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texcoco">Texcoco</a>, Zumpango and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xaltocan">Xaltocan</a> and the freshwater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-xochimilco">Lake Xochimilco</a> it formed the lake system of the Basin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, home to Mesoamerican cultures including the Toltecs and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>. Its water stayed fresh largely because of the artesian springs that lined its south shore. Lake Chalco and Lake Xochimilco were the original habitat of the axolotl, an amphibian now critically endangered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Fresh water and plentiful fish made the Chalco region one of the most populous parts of the basin early in its history. Around 500 BCE it had about 21,810 inhabitants, the highest figure in the basin at the time, and the highest density, 109.1 people per hectare. After the rise of Teotihuacan its population fell sharply, but around 1400 it grew again, from 75.0 to 150 people per hectare.</p><p>That growth is thought to be closely linked to chinampa farming. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinampa">Chinampas</a> existed on a limited scale from about 300 to 1350, and in their later form are said to have been introduced to the Chalco-Xochimilco lake bed some time after 1400. The Aztec ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itzcoatl">Itzcoatl</a> conquered the southern lakes and used them for chinampa development, since the Triple Alliance needed large amounts of food to sustain the growth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, and the drier parts of the valley suited this farming less well. Late in his reign Itzcoatl built a causeway linking Tenochtitlan to towns near Xochimilco. After the Spanish conquest the chinampa beds lost the support of Aztec infrastructure, fell into disuse and were largely abandoned.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Efforts to drain Lake Chalco and its sister lakes, against periodic flooding and to gain land, began in the Aztec era and went on into the 20th century. A land speculator's draining of the lake in the late 1860s provoked a revolt of tenant farmers organised by Julio López Chávez, which the federal government put down. Of the old lakes only a diminished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-xochimilco">Lake Xochimilco</a> and the Lake of Zumpango survive.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lake Chalco” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Chalco developed around the eastern shore of <b>Lake Chalco</b> and the slopes of the volcanoes Popocatépetl and Iztaccíhuatl.</blockquote><a href="/state/chalco">Chalco</a></li></ul></section>
